sed/awk aren't a good fit here - Python's block structure is indentation-defined and nested, which line-oriented regex tools handle poorly and fragilely. Since you already know Python, use the ast module instead: parse each file with ast.parse, walk the tree for ast.ClassDef nodes, check whether any of node.bases has an id matching your base class name, print the file path once, then print the class header and, for each ast.FunctionDef/ast.AsyncFunctionDef in node.body, print just its name without the body. Rough shape:
import ast, pathlib
for path in pathlib.Path().rglob('*.py'):
    tree = ast.parse(path.read_text())
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        if isinstance(node, ast.ClassDef) and any(getattr(b, 'id', None) == 'BaseClass' for b in node.bases):
            print(path)
            print('class', node.name)
            for item in node.body:
                if isinstance(item, (ast.FunctionDef, ast.AsyncFunctionDef)):
                    kind = 'async def' if isinstance(item, ast.AsyncFunctionDef) else 'def'
                    print(' ', kind, item.name)
This correctly handles multi-file, multi-class, nested async/sync methods - something sed/awk can't parse reliably given Python's whitespace-significant grammar.

Instead of overriding every mutating method by hand, loop over their names and assign the same raising function to all of them; it's much less repetitive and won't silently miss a method if the base class changes:
class ImmutableModifyError(Exception): pass
def _blocked(self, *a, **k):
    raise ImmutableModifyError(self.err)
class ImmutableList(list):
    def __init__(self, err='', *argv):
        self.err = err
        super().__init__(*argv)
for name in ('append','extend','clear','insert','pop','remove','sort','reverse','__setitem__','__delitem__','__iadd__'):
    setattr(ImmutableList, name, _blocked)
You could make this fully generic with a factory function that builds such a subclass for any mutable base (dict, set, etc.), reused across types. Note this still isn't airtight - other mutation paths some built-ins expose could bypass it - so for true immutability, prefer composition around an internal tuple with a read-only view, rather than inheriting from a mutable built-in and blocking every mutator after the fact.
